# G022 — RSS Feed Creator

*v2. See `rss-feed.md` for prose: from string to document (first
structured content with schema), publication channel as broadcast,
serialisation as communication across boundaries, RSS items as
vault-note analogue.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Vault Publication Feed

[vault-publication-feed @tag
    IN ORDER TO
        assemble vault notes tagged @tag into an RSS document
        emit the serialised XML to subscribers via dpn-reader
        and round-trip through parse to verify the schema

    @items <- [vault[find-notes {tag: @tag}]]
               mapped to [rss[item-from-note {note: @n}]]

    @feed <- [rss[feed
                    {title: "Vault / " + @tag,
                     link: "https://vault.dpn/feeds/" + @tag,
                     description: "vault notes tagged " + @tag,
                     items: @items}]]

    @xml    <- [rss[to-xml {feed: @feed}]]
    @parsed <- [rss[from-xml {xml: @xml}]]

    @parsed
        where equal to @feed
            -> [dpn-reader[publish {feed-xml: @xml}]]
        otherwise
            -> #round-trip-failed
]

---

## Verb Surface

[rss
    feed                 {title, link, description, items}
    item                 {title, link, description, pub-date}
    item-from-note       {note}
    to-xml               {feed}                    ;; serialise
    from-xml             {xml}                      ;; parse
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: round-trip serialisation `where` parse ∘ emit = id

[rss[from-xml {xml: [rss[to-xml {feed: @f}]]}]
    where result equal to @f
    IN ORDER TO
        establish the serialisation contract: wire-form round-trips
        exactly, so agents on different machines can exchange feeds
        without losing structure
]

Serialisation IS how agents communicate across boundaries. G022
introduces the primitive: take a structured value, flatten to a
string, transmit, reconstruct. Same discipline G085 Quiz, G090 Zip,
G100 Versioning extend across the Files category.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: one-to-many broadcast contract

[rss[feed]]
    IN ORDER TO
        publish one document that many consumers read independently
        without the publisher knowing who they are
        and without consumers coordinating with each other

    the feed is the shared contract — "here's structured content in
    a format we all agree on" — the simplest distributed protocol
    in the Rosetta Stone, and the shape every pub-sub channel uses
]

---

## Demo

[demo
    @f <- [rss[feed
                 {title: "Vault Weekly",
                  link: "https://vault.dpn/weekly",
                  description: "weekly digest",
                  items: [[rss[item
                                {title: "Rosetta Stone second pass",
                                 link: "https://vault.dpn/rosetta-stone",
                                 description: "130 .dpn files",
                                 pub-date: "2026-04-21"}]]]}]]

    @xml <- [rss[to-xml {feed: @f}]]
    -> [rss[from-xml {xml: @xml}]]
       is equal to @f                        ;; -> true
]
